Flood Monitoring System Repository Guide
=======================================

This repository contains a full-stack flood monitoring and prediction web app based on the supplied GLOC 2026 research paper. It is designed as both an operational dashboard and a living reproduction of the paper methodology, figures, charts, tables, and seeded results.

Quick Start
-----------

Run from the project root:

    cd C:\software_engineering\flood-monitoring-system
    docker compose up --build

Then open:

    Frontend: http://localhost:3000
    Backend API docs: http://localhost:8000/docs
    Health check: http://localhost:8000/health

Stop the app with Ctrl+C, then:

    docker compose down

Top-Level Files
---------------

- docker-compose.yml
  Runs PostgreSQL 15, the FastAPI backend on port 8000, and the React frontend on port 3000.

- .env.example
  Documents required runtime variables, including DATABASE_URL, COPERNICUS_USER, COPERNICUS_PASSWORD, and VITE_API_URL.

- .gitignore
  Keeps local dependencies, generated Python files, environment files, model pickles, PDFs, local DB files, and QA screenshots out of git.

- README.md
  User-facing setup and project overview.

- .readme
  This detailed repository map for maintainers and reviewers.

Backend
-------

Location:

    backend/

Main technologies:

- FastAPI
- SQLAlchemy
- Alembic
- Pydantic
- PostgreSQL
- NumPy, SciPy, scikit-image, scikit-learn, XGBoost, rasterio
- Open-Meteo integration through httpx

Important files:

- backend/app/main.py
  Creates the FastAPI app, configures CORS, includes all routers, and seeds the database on startup through a FastAPI lifespan handler.

- backend/app/database.py
  Configures SQLAlchemy engine/session handling and initializes tables.

- backend/app/config.py
  Loads environment configuration through pydantic-settings.

- backend/seed_db.py
  Idempotently seeds the paper results, initial regions, events, and alerts.

- backend/alembic/
  Contains Alembic configuration and the initial migration.

- backend/tests/test_backend.py
  Backend tests for seeded paper data, prediction shape, feature extraction, labeling, and index math.

Backend API Routes
------------------

The route modules live in:

    backend/app/api/routes/

Exposed endpoints:

- POST /ingest
  Accepts country, lat/lon, buffer, and date range. Uses Copernicus credentials if available; otherwise returns deterministic simulated ingest metadata.

- POST /predict
  Returns flood_probability, risk_level, classification, and confidence.

- POST /forecast
  Returns a five-day Open-Meteo operational forecast with flood_likelihood, risk_level, precipitation_mm, and soil_moisture.

- GET /paper-results
  Returns seeded paper reproduction data: dataset stats, model metrics, ablation results, confusion matrices, sensitivity analysis, key features, and paper notes.

- GET /events
  Returns seeded/recent flood monitoring events.

- GET /alerts
  Returns active alert records.

- GET /regions
  Returns configured monitoring regions.

Backend Services
----------------

Location:

    backend/app/services/

- preprocessing.py
  Implements QA60 cloud masking, NDWI/NDVI computation, normalization, 64x64 tiling, 61-feature extraction, and flood labeling.

- classifier.py
  Loads backend/app/ml/xgboost_model.pkl if present. If no model exists, uses a deterministic fallback so the app still demos end-to-end.

- forecaster.py
  Calls Open-Meteo and maps precipitation/soil moisture into flood likelihood. Falls back to deterministic data if the weather request fails.

- satellite.py
  Contains the Copernicus ingest shell and simulated fallback behavior.

- paper_data.py
  Contains the authoritative seeded paper values used by the UI and API.

- patch_visualizer.py
  Provides deterministic metadata helpers for simulated paper visuals.

- risk.py
  Shared Low/Medium/High risk thresholds.

Frontend
--------

Location:

    frontend/

Main technologies:

- React 18
- TypeScript
- Vite
- Tailwind CSS
- Recharts
- Leaflet / react-leaflet
- lucide-react icons

Important files:

- frontend/src/App.tsx
  Top-level navigation shell for Dashboard, Predictor, Forecast, History, and Methodology.

- frontend/src/api/client.ts
  Fetch client and shared response types for backend communication.

- frontend/src/vite-env.d.ts
  Vite type shim for import.meta.env support in TypeScript builds.

- frontend/src/styles/index.css
  Tailwind entrypoint and shared utility styles.

Frontend Pages
--------------

Location:

    frontend/src/pages/

- Dashboard.tsx
  Interactive map, selected-region prediction, flood gauge, alert banner, and recent events table.

- Predictor.tsx
  Country/date input flow that calls POST /predict.

- Forecast.tsx
  Five-day Open-Meteo operational forecast view. This is intentionally labeled as an app extension, not a paper result.

- History.tsx
  Searchable flood-event table with CSV export.

- Methodology.tsx
  Centerpiece paper reproduction page with anchor navigation and all required figures/tables.

Frontend Components
-------------------

Location:

    frontend/src/components/

Operational dashboard components:

- Map.tsx
- CountrySelector.tsx
- FloodGauge.tsx
- ForecastChart.tsx
- AlertBanner.tsx
- EventsTable.tsx

Paper reproduction components:

- WorkflowDiagram.tsx
  Reproduces Fig. 1 as a four-phase workflow.

- NDWIMask.tsx
  Reproduces Fig. 2 with deterministic simulated raw and NDWI-masked panels. The fallback texture was improved to avoid duplicate-looking diagonal patterns.

- PatchGrid.tsx
  Reproduces Fig. 3 with 16 deterministic 64x64 patch thumbnails: 8 flood and 8 no-flood. The fallback generator now uses varied deterministic noise/blobs so thumbnails do not look duplicated.

- PixelDistribution.tsx
  Reproduces Fig. 4 with June/July/August NDWI distributions and the NDWI > 0.0 threshold.

- ROCCurveChart.tsx
  Reproduces Fig. 5 with seeded ROC curves and AUC labels.

- DatasetTable.tsx
  Reproduces Table 2.

- ModelMetricsTable.tsx
  Reproduces Table A1.

- AblationTable.tsx
  Reproduces Table 3.

Seeded Paper Values
-------------------

The app treats Table A1 and Table 3 as authoritative where the PDF prose conflicts with structured values.

Key seeded values:

- June 1, 2024: 2,630 patches, 41.8% flooded
- July 1, 2024: 2,249 patches, 13.3% flooded
- August 1, 2024: 1,889 patches, 3.9% flooded
- XGBoost: ROC-AUC 0.9985, Accuracy 99.4%, Precision 86.7%, Recall 98.6%, F1 0.923
- FULL ablation row: ROC-AUC 1.0000, Accuracy 1.0000, F1 1.0000
- NO_WATER_FRACTION: ROC-AUC 0.9998, F1 0.9910
- NO_NDWI: ROC-AUC 0.9899, F1 0.9639

Testing And Verification
------------------------

Backend tests:

    docker compose exec -T backend pytest

Known passing result after the latest verification:

    4 passed

Build checks:

    docker compose build frontend
    docker compose build backend
    docker compose up --build

Verified behavior:

- Frontend production build passes.
- Backend image builds with the ML dependencies.
- db, backend, and frontend containers start successfully.
- http://localhost:8000/health returns {"status":"ok"}.
- /paper-results returns exact seeded values.
- POST /predict works for Bangladesh.
- POST /forecast returns five days.
- Browser QA passed for Dashboard, Predictor, Forecast, History, and Methodology.

Demo Flow
---------

1. Open http://localhost:3000.
2. Dashboard: show map, Bangladesh risk, flood gauge, alert, and events.
3. Predictor: run Bangladesh for 2024-08-01 and show probability/classification/confidence.
4. Forecast: show the five-day Open-Meteo extension chart.
5. History: show event table and CSV export.
6. Methodology: scroll through Fig. 1-5, Table 2, Table A1, and Table 3.

Fallbacks And Limitations
-------------------------

- Copernicus credentials are optional. Without them, ingest uses simulated metadata.
- The XGBoost pickle is optional. Without backend/app/ml/xgboost_model.pkl, prediction uses a deterministic fallback.
- Paper visuals use deterministic simulated satellite textures unless real tiles are later wired in.
- The current frontend build has a non-blocking Vite chunk-size warning due to map/chart libraries.
